INDEXING & AGGREGATION

=>Indexing and aggregation are two crucial concepts in MongoDB that help with performance optimization and data manipulation.

=>Indexing in MongoDB

Indexing is a technique used to improve the speed of data retrieval operations on a database. In MongoDB, indexing creates a data structure that improves the speed of searches on a collection. Without an index, MongoDB must scan the entire collection to select documents that match the query, which can be inefficient.

=>Types of Indexes

1. Single Field Index: An index on a single field within a document.

   -------------------------------
   db.collection.createIndex({ fieldName: 1 }) // 1 for ascending order, -1 for descending
   -------------------------------

    Note:
    ->check Indexes
    -------------------------------
    db.collection.getIndexes({key:value})
    -------------------------------
    ->db.customer.getIndexes({name:'age_1'})
    -------------------------------

    ->Drop Indexes

    ------------------------------
    db.collection.dropIndex({key:value})
    ------------------------------
    db.customer.dropIndex({age:1})
    ------------------------------
    
2. Compound Index: An index on multiple fields.

   -------------------------------
   db.collection.createIndex({ field1: 1, field2: -1 })
   -------------------------------
   db.customer.createIndex({age:1,gender:1})
   -------------------------------

3. Multikey Index: Automatically created when indexing an array field. Each unique value in the array creates a separate index entry.

4. Text Index: Used for text-searching capabilities.
   
   -------------------------------
   db.collection.createIndex({ fieldName: "text" })
   -------------------------------
   db.customer.createIndex({age:"text",gender:"text"})
   -------------------------------

   check:
   ---------------------------------
    db.data.findOne({$text:{$search:"lacus"}})
   ---------------------------------

5. Geospatial Index: Used for location-based data.
   -------------------------------
   db.collection.createIndex({ location: "2dsphere" })
   -------------------------------

6. Wildcard Index: Allows indexing of fields within an entire document structure, regardless of their names.
   ---------------------------
   db.collection.createIndex({ "$**": 1 })
   ---------------------------

=>Best Practices for Indexing

    -> Use indexes to speed up frequently used queries, especially those with sort operations and filters.
    -> Avoid over-indexing, as it can lead to higher write times due to index updates.
    -> Analyze and monitor query performance to identify which indexes to create or remove.

=>Aggregation in MongoDB

    Aggregation in MongoDB is a powerful framework for data processing and transformation. It allows for operations like filtering, grouping, and summarizing data.
    Syntex:
    ------------------------------------------
    db.collections.aggregate(pipeline,)
    ------------------------------------------
    example:

    1. match gender of group
    ------------------------------------------
    db.teachers.aggregate([{$match:{gender:male}}])
    ------------------------------------------

    2. The name field uses the $push operator to add the name field for each document in the group to an array.
    --------------------------------------------
    (a)db.teachers.aggregate([{$group:{_id:"$age",names:{$push:"$name"}}}])
    -------------------------------------------- 

    --------------------------------------------
    (b)db.teachers.aggregate([{$group:{_id:"$age",allDoc:{$push:"$$ROOT"}}}])
    -------------------------------------------- 
=>Aggregation Framework


    The aggregation framework includes the following pipeline stages:

    1. $match: Filters documents based on a specified condition.
    2. $group: Groups documents by some specified identifier.
    3. $sort: Sorts the documents.
    4. $project: Reshapes each document in the stream.
    5. $limit: Limits the number of documents.
    6. $skip: Skips over the specified nuoptionsmber of documents.
    7. $lookup: Performs a left outer join to a different collection.

=>Example of an Aggregation Pipeline

Here's an example of how to use the aggregation framework:

------------------------------
db.orders.aggregate([
    { $match: { status: "completed" } },
    { $group: { _id: "$customerId", total: { $sum: "$amount" } } },
    { $sort: { total: -1 } },
    { $limit: 5 }
])
-------------------------------

In this example, we:
    -> Match documents where `status` is "completed".
    -> Group the results by `customerId` and sum the `amount`.
    -> Sort the results by total amount in descending order.
    -> Limit the output to the top 5 customers.

=>Performance Considerations

    -> Use indexing to optimize query performance, especially for aggregation operations.
    -> Consider using the `$explain` method to analyze your aggregation queries and understand their performance.
    -> Keep your documents sized appropriately; very large documents can slow down performance during aggregation.

=>Conclusion

    ->Indexing and aggregation are powerful tools in MongoDB that can significantly enhance data retrieval and processing performance. By effectively utilizing these features, you can optimize your data architecture for better efficiency and scalability. Always monitor performance closely to adapt your strategies as necessary!